  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 AM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nShowers/storms currently over western Kansas will continue to move \nslowly eastward and look to linger this morning over south central \nKansas. Not expecting strong or severe storms with this activity. \nMeanwhile we will be watching for two possible scenarios for storm \nre-development later today and tonight. First scenario, is for \nstorms to potentially develop along a surface frontal boundary near \nthe Kansas/Oklahoma border during the late afternoon/evening \nhours(this will be predicated on what this morning's activity does \nto the effective surface boundary). If storms can develop along\nthis surface boundary, moderate instability combined with \ndecent wind shear profile aloft favors some storms reaching \nsevere levels. Inverted V-type sounding profile will create high\nDCAPE values over 1000, and good directional shear in the \n700-300mb layer supports a few severe storms capable of \nproducing up to golf ball size hail and damaging winds up to \n70mph. The second scenario seems more likely, once again seeing \na signal for elevated storms to develop tonight as low level jet\nincreases moisture transport into 700mb baroclinic zone. The \nmain risk from these night-time storms would be large hail early\non then possible heavy rainfall. The combination of precipitable\nwater values increasing over south central/southeast Kansas \novernight, and low level jet focus remaining more upstream \nfavors a prolonged period of showers/storms tonight into early \nFriday morning. \n\nNo real changes for the Saturday through Tuesday period as models \ncontinue to show upper level high pressure center being stretched \neastward across the southern plains. This will cause the heat dome \nto build over Kansas with triple digit daytime highs likely to occur \nover central/south central Kansas. Model ensemble probabilities of \n100 degrees or greater for daytime highs Sunday-Tuesday are at 70% \nor higher for those same locations. Some long range models are \nshowing potential for storms to develop along a sagging frontal \nboundary near central Kansas for Tuesday and Wednesday.",
